\node Test, ANVL Test Plugin
@chapter Test, ANVL Plugin

@code{anvl_test} is a plugin for running Erlang/OTP test suites.

Test suites are discovered automatically in the OTP applications of the
project, and executed on isolated peer nodes.

@section Writing test suites

A test suite is an Erlang module that implements the
@code{anvl_test_suite} behavior:

@verbatim
-module(my_suite).
-behavior(anvl_test_suite).

-include_lib("anvl_test/include/anvl_test.hrl").

-test(some_test).
some_test(run, #{instance := _}) ->
  ok.
@end verbatim

Including @file{anvl_test.hrl} applies a parse transform that collects
the @code{-test} attributes into a module attribute
(@code{anvl_test_tests}) and exports the test functions.

@section Test case instances

The same test function can be run against multiple @i{instances}.
Default implementation of the @code{instances/0} callback returns
@code{[default]}.

@section Test clusters

Test cases that need a cluster of their own,
e.g. for testing distributed functionality,
can use the @code{anvl_test_cluster} fixture.
It creates a @code{familiar} cluster on the node where the test case runs,
in a network that is derived from the network settings of the test case,
so clusters of the test cases running in parallel never collide:

@verbatim
-test(foo).
foo(fixtures, #{instance := default}) ->
  [{anvl_test_cluster, #{}}];
foo(run, #{instance := default}) ->
  Cluster = anvl_test_cluster:get_cluster(),
  {ok, Site} = familiar:create_site(Cluster, site1),
  {ok, _Node} = familiar:start_site(Site).
@end verbatim

The @code{net} and @code{subnet} parameters of the cluster
are set by the fixture and cannot be overridden.
The rest of the cluster configuration
(@code{fixtures}, @code{peer}, etc.)
is taken from the fixture configuration,
and the cluster ID is generated by the fixture when not set explicitly.
The auto shutdown of the cluster is always disabled,
because it is created by a short-lived RPC process.
The cluster is destroyed when the node of the test case is stopped.

@lee{anvl_test,ANVL Test}
